speechtext:
I ought almost to apologize to the House for throwing an apple of discord into this debate. Let me say that I do not feel called upon to indulge in any lofty panegyrics of British Columbia apples, although I hold my own opinion, as other gentlemen hold theirs. But as a matter of fact we who know the results recognize frankly that the Nova Scotia Gravenstein, the Ontario Spy, the Quebec Fameuse and the Cox's Orange or Jonathan of my own province-any of these apples, grown under the best conditions, will satisfy any taste, perhaps even that of the Minister of Agriculture. I think it is a mis-Mr. CROSBY.	;
take to make these comparisons. There is one common ground on which we can all stand, which interests those who are not apple growers, but apple consumers. By striving in the best way we can to give the consumer what he wants we shall hasten that millennium * described, by Oliver Wendell Holmes-and this may appeal to the lawyers in the House-a millennium which will arrive;
When legislators keep the law,
And banks dispense with bolts and locks,
And berries, whortle, rasp and straw, Grow bigger downwards through the box.
Whatever else we dispute about, I think we in British Columbia, not only so far as flavour goes, but by the excellence of our packing, have the satisfaction of getting our fruit to the consumers in such shape that we can give pointers in this respect to most of the other province^. I regret these sectional differences, because in my opinion we should all join together in making this industry a national one, and give it predominance in the markets of the world, and I credit the- hon. minister with having the like intention. ,
